     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg per day. The usual maximum is about 2,000\u20132,550 mg per day, and a single dose is typically no more than 1,000 mg. At this level there is a serious risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity. It may be a typo. For example, it could be meant as 1700 mg total per day, or 850 mg twice daily.\n\n2. **You aren't a clinician.** Medication orders should come from a licensed prescriber, or be verified by one, and that matters even more when the dose looks wrong.\n\nPlease ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose and frequency. Once I have a corrected dose, or a prescriber confirms this one is intentional and gives the clinical reason, I can enter it. For example, I could enter 850 mg orally twice daily with meals, if that's what they intend."
      }
     ],
